               "opportunityCheck":  "The exposure score of 5 looks clearly too low. In Q3 the AI segment brought in $7.56B of $14.58B revenue (about 52%) and rents Colossus compute to Google and Anthropic, so I would raise exposure the most. Market growth and optionality fit what I found. The position score is fair: Grok still trails the leading labs, and the compute-rental business is new. A low bottleneck score also makes sense, because power and permits are the scarce input, not SpaceX, as the Arizona and Texas setbacks show.",
               "technical":  "The stock has only 113 trading days of history since its 12 Jun 2026 listing, so EMA 200 is not available. Trend: price at $127.79 is about 11% below both the EMA 50 ($143.98) and EMA 100 ($143.73). An EMA is an average of recent closes that gives more weight to the latest days. The two lines are almost touching, with EMA 50 barely above EMA 100, so the medium-term trend has gone flat and is tilting down. Momentum: MACD (12/26/9, a trend-momentum gauge) is -3.12, just below its signal line at -2.98. The histogram is slightly negative at -0.14, a fresh but still weak bearish turn. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge; under 30 is oversold) is 38.5, which is weak but not oversold. Volatility: price closed right on the lower Bollinger Band at $127.35 (middle $139.18, upper $151), a sign the move is stretched. Price sits in the lower fifth of its 52-week range of $104.83–$225.64. Volume of 124.0M was about 1.2x the 20-day average of 103.5M, so real selling backed the 20 Nov drop. Support is the $127.35 band, then $104.83. Resistance is near $139 and $144.",
               "fundamental":  "SpaceX now has three businesses: Starlink earns the cash, launch is strategic, and AI drives growth. In Q3 2026 (reported 20 Oct), revenue was $14.58B (+161%), operating income $2.8B and net income $4.4B, helped by about $1.6B of non-operating gains. Connectivity revenue rose 69% to $5.25B with $2.37B of operating income. AI revenue jumped to $7.56B with a $431M profit; this includes Cursor, whose $60B acquisition closed in the quarter. Launch brought in $1.77B, about break-even. The weak spot is cash: capex was $19.6B in one quarter. In the AI value chain, SpaceX is now both a compute landlord (Google, Anthropic) and a model builder (Grok). Its AI returns depend on getting power and permits for the $74B Arizona and Texas build-out.",
               "news":  [
                            "20 Oct 2026: Q3 beat estimates, with revenue of $14.58B vs $12.72B expected and adjusted EPS of $0.45 vs $0.13. Shares rose about 8% after hours from a $147.50 close.",
                            "5 Nov 2026: Shares fell as much as 11% after Democrats won both chambers of Congress and Gina Hinojosa won the Texas governor\u0027s race. That raised the risk of contract oversight and pauses on new data centers.",
                            "17 Nov 2026: Cameron County, Texas, voted 3-2 against holding a public hearing on a 10-year tax break for SpaceX\u0027s $24B AI data center near Brownsville.",
